1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,997
One hidden door could let a stranger take over your web server.

2
00:00:04,047 --> 00:00:05,217
Meet Priya.

3
00:00:05,267 --> 00:00:10,959
Her online shop runs on Apache, one of the most popular web servers in the world.

4
00:00:11,009 --> 00:00:12,675
Oh, hi Muja!

5
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:16,370
Every visitor talks to her server through HTTP/2.

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:20,950
Think of it as one fast lane that carries lots of requests at once.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,967
The part of Apache that runs this lane is called mod_http2.

8
00:00:26,017 --> 00:00:27,902
And it had a memory bug.

9
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:31,682
Send it the right messages, and the server gets confused.

10
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:34,726
Then an attacker could run their own code on it.

11
00:00:34,776 --> 00:00:36,778
Apache rated it critical.

12
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:45,625
Versions 2.4.55 to 2.4.70 are affected, when HTTP/2 is switched on.

13
00:00:45,675 --> 00:00:47,015
The fix is out.

14
00:00:47,065 --> 00:00:50,347
Priya updates to 2.4.71.

15
00:00:50,397 --> 00:00:55,589
Then she restarts the server, because the old code keeps running until she does.

16
00:00:55,639 --> 00:00:57,145
Can't patch today?

17
00:00:57,195 --> 00:00:59,911
Switch HTTP/2 off for now.

18
00:00:59,961 --> 00:01:04,438
No attacks were reported yet, but a public test lab already exists.

19
00:01:04,488 --> 00:01:06,042
So patch this week.

20
00:01:06,092 --> 00:01:08,339
The full story is on my blog.
